{"records":[{"id":"wkd5wp9zwk","title":"data for CasPER, a method for directed evolution in genomic contexts using mutagenesis and CRISPR/Cas9","description":"This is the raw NGS sequencing data as python analysis for the paper thats currently titled:\nCasPER, a method for directed evolution in genomic contexts using mutagenesis and CRISPR/Cas9","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-06-14T11:58:27.336Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/wkd5wp9zwk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/wkd5wp9zwk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lasse Ebdrup","last_name":"Pedersen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Yeast","Data Analysis","Next Generation Sequencing"],"journal_issn":["10967176"]},{"id":"ztg7pxr6ch","title":"STT3A is essential for Wnt signaling and represents a target for cancers driven by RNF43 deficiency","description":"This dataset contains the original raw and analyzed data supporting the manuscript “STT3A is essential for Wnt signaling and represents a target for cancers driven by RNF43 deficiency”. The study identifies STT3A as an essential regulator of the Wnt signaling pathway and a potential therapeutic target for RNF43-deficient cancers. Included data were generated from key experiments, including Wnt reporter assays (DDT), Western blotting (WB), and CRISPR screen, and other experiments central to the study","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-13T11:26:58.874Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ztg7pxr6ch","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ztg7pxr6ch"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zhengjin","last_name":"He","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"sxrvzkr4hh","title":"Pooled in vitro and in vivo CRISPR-Cas9 screening identifies tumor suppressors in human colon organoids","description":"UMI validation screen, original data from in vivo tumor suppressor CRISPR/Cas9 screening experiment. Figure 6.\nExperimental setup: 281 tumor suppressor gRNAs, positive controls, neutral controls and gRNAs targeting essential genes were introduced into the CRISPR-UMI lentivirus (Michlits et al., 2017). Transduced AK organoids (APC-KO and KRASG12D) were transplanted subcutaneously in NSG mice followed by barcode sequencing of the tumor pool (from 10 mice) and 5 individual tumors. Clone and read number were determined by next generation sequencing. The conventional gRNA enrichment analysis was compared to UMI-CRISPR-screening with outlier removal.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-04-20T15:46:17.868Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/sxrvzkr4hh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/sxrvzkr4hh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Henner","last_name":"Farin","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y8dndzn3mg","title":"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication","description":"These data are part of the Supplemental Items associated with the manuscript entitled \"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication\". These files include Supplementary Data file 1-4.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-16T10:34:13.579Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y8dndzn3mg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y8dndzn3mg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Limeng","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","Screening","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4mb5kjywkj","title":"Western Blot raw data of the manuscript titled \"In vivo CRISPR knockout screen identifies p47 as a suppressor for HER2+ breast cancer metastasis by regulating NEMO trafficking and autophagy flux\"","description":"The raw images of WB is for the reviewers' request when reviewing the manuscript titled \"In vivo CRISPR knockout screen identifies p47 as a suppressor for HER2+ breast cancer metastasis by regulating NEMO trafficking and autophagy flux\".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-12-04T00:26:32.621Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4mb5kjywkj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4mb5kjywkj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mingang","last_name":"Hao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gfcn2wmrpf","title":"Dataset for the paper entitled \"A genetic map of the response to DNA damage in human cells\" by Olivieri M et al. Cell 2020","description":"We used pooled CRISPR/Cas9 screens in the human RPE1-hTERT p53-/- cell line against 27 genotoxic agents. The Dataset herein make the primary data available. The files are:\n\n* Additional genes of interest (discussion and data relating to TMEM2, ESD, USP37, PHF12, BTAF1 and DRAP1)\n* Supplementary Raw Data: Data used to make all the graphs in the manuscript\n* Folder \"CRISPR screen readcount files\". Readcounts for the screens undertaken as part of this study.\n* Folder \"Raw images for immunoblots\": uncropped images for all the blots in the manuscript\n* Folder: \"Additional QC analyses of CRISPR screens\": Additional analyses that measure the quality of the screens using Presion-Recall curves of essential genes and an estimate of genes that scored as hits due to batch effects.\n* Folder \"Data_Analysis_Code\" R markdown file along with data files\n* File: \"Additional Genes of Interests\" contains a short discussion of genes we found noteworthy after analysis of our screen data.\n\nVersion 2 note: this version differs from Version 1 by a single file (FDRPos_31screens.csv) which has a tab error fixed.\n\n\n\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-07-07T14:26:40.176Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gfcn2wmrpf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gfcn2wmrpf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Durocher","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michele","last_name":"Olivieri","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Quality","Primary Data"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"65pfcj4j4m","title":"Protocol of CRISPR-Cas9 knockout screens for identifying ferroptosis regulators","description":"Ferroptosis, an iron-dependent programmed cell death triggered by excessive lipid peroxidation, has shown promising therapeutic potentials in human diseases. Here, we describe a protocol of a CRISPR-Cas9 loss-of-function screen to identify regulators in response to different inducers of ferroptosis. We emphasize the steps of library amplification, drug treatment, high-throughput sequencing preparation, and bioinformatics analysis using Model-based Analysis of Genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 Knockout (MAGeCK). We also present a method to discover the regulators of ferroptosis and verify the potential targets efficiently.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-10-24T01:46:48.793Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/65pfcj4j4m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/65pfcj4j4m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xin","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wei","last_name":"Gu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR/Cas9","Ferroptosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"d5vw9sjcrv","title":"A target expression threshold dictates invader defense  and autoimmunity by CRISPR-Cas13","description":"Immune systems must recognize and clear foreign invaders without eliciting autoimmunity. CRISPR-Cas immune systems in prokaryotes manage this task by following two criteria: extensive guide:target complementarity and a defined target-flanking motif. Here we report an additional requirement for RNA-targeting CRISPR-Cas13 systems: expression of the target transcript exceeding a threshold. This finding is based on targeting endogenous non-essential transcripts, which rarely elicited dormancy through collateral RNA degradation. Instead, eliciting dormancy required over-expressing targeted transcripts above a threshold. A genome-wide screen confirmed target expression levels as the principal determinant of cytotoxic autoimmunity and revealed that the threshold shifts with the guide:target pair. This expression threshold ensured defense against a lytic bacteriophage yet allowed tolerance of a targeted beneficial gene expressed from an invading plasmid. These findings establish target expression levels as a third criterion for immune defense by RNA-targeting CRISPR-Cas systems, buffering against autoimmunity and distinguishing pathogenic and benign invaders.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-06-08T11:55:48.983Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/d5vw9sjcrv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/d5vw9sjcrv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Elena","last_name":"Vialetto","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yanying","last_name":"Yu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Scott","last_name":"Collins","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Katharina","last_name":"Wandera","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lars","last_name":"Barquist","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chase","last_name":"Beisel","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Analysis","Data Access"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"75brhkh25r","title":"A genome-wide hepatocyte CRISPR-CAS9 screen to identify host factors essential for optimal Plasmodium liver stage development. ","description":"To identify host genes critical for Plasmodium LS development, we used the GeCKOv2 sgRNA pool to generate a whole-genome knockout library in HepG2-CD81 cells (Sanjana et al., 2014; Shalem et al., 2014). HepG2-CD81 cells were transduced with lentivirus containing the pooled GeCKOv2 sgRNA library of 114,123 sgRNAs targeting 19,031 protein-coding genes (~6 sgRNAs/gene) and selected in puromycin for 5-7 days. To evaluate sgRNA diversity in the HepG2-CD81-GeCKOv2 library, we PCR-amplified the integrated sgRNA cassettes from genomic DNA extracted from transduced cells and subjected the amplified library to Illumina sequencing. At the gene level, sgRNAs targeting all but 15 of 19,031 (99.92%) protein-coding genes were observed. Twelve to fourteen days after post-transduction, we infected forty million puromycin-resistant cells with a green fluorescent protein (GFP) expressing-Plasmodium yoelii at a multiplicity of infection (MOI) of 0.3. After 24 h of infection, cells were sorted into infected and bystander cell populations by GFP signal intensity with fluorescence-activated cell sorting (FACS). Separately, a parallel culture of uninfected cells was also maintained to normalize the sgRNA frequency distributions. We obtained four independent biological replicates with library generation and sequencing occurring in parallel. Genes with significantly enriched sgRNAs were identified for both the bystander and infected populations when compared to uninfected cells. Cells that harbor genetic alterations restricting P. yoelii development (i.e., sgRNAs that target host genes important for infection) were expected to be enriched in the uninfected bin; we termed this group ‘putative positive regulators of infection’. We categorized sgRNAs enriched in the infected cells as ‘putative negative regulators of infection’. In this initial screen, we identified 242 sgRNAs that were statistically enriched in infected or bystander groups after accounting for multiple hypotheses. There were 67 sgRNAs significantly enriched in the infected cells compared to uninfected cells and 175 genes were significantly enriched in the bystander bin relative to the uninfected bin. \n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-27T07:31:54.012Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/75brhkh25r","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/75brhkh25r"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kamalakannan","last_name":"Vijayan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nadia","last_name":"Arang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ling ","last_name":"Wei","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Robert","last_name":"Morrison","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rechel ","last_name":"Geiger","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rachael ","last_name":"Parks","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Adam J ","last_name":"Lewis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fred D ","last_name":"Mast ","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alyse N ","last_name":"Douglass","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Heather S ","last_name":"Kain","orcid":null},{"first_name":"John D","last_name":"Aitchison ","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jarrod S ","last_name":"Johnson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alan ","last_name":"Aderem","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alexis","last_name":"Kaushansky","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nnxh5d35nd","title":"Genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 screen identified KLF11 as a druggable suppressor for sarcoma cancer stem cells","description":"The original, unprocessed data alongside the paper","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-05-31T01:39:45.722Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nnxh5d35nd","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nnxh5d35nd"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Le","last_name":"Qu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":106317,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":89,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":106317,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":105947,"restricted":54,"embargoed":316},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":1690},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":508},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":386},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":345},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":326},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":319},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":319},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":290},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":199},{"id":"https://ror.org/00k4n6c32","name":"European Commission","count":180}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":104049},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1689},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":135},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":111},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":60},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":59},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":42},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":31},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":25},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":24}